# Lesson 31 — ANOVA, Standard Error, and Mixed Statistics Practice

## ANOVA
ANOVA tests whether there is evidence that at least one mean differs across three or more groups.

- Null hypothesis: all group means are equal.
- Alternative hypothesis: at least one group mean differs.

A significant ANOVA result does not identify which specific groups differ; post-hoc comparisons may be needed.

## Standard Error
Standard error measures the uncertainty of a sample estimate.

For a sample mean:
```text
SE = s / sqrt(n)
```

As sample size increases, standard error generally decreases.

## Mixed Practice Checklist
Be ready to distinguish:
- Standard deviation vs. standard error
- Confidence interval vs. p-value
- Statistical significance vs. business significance
- Correlation vs. causation
- Type I vs. Type II errors
- t-test vs. ANOVA

## Interview Takeaway
For statistics interviews, define the concept briefly, explain when it is used, and connect it to the decision the analyst is trying to make.
